Date: Fri, 17 Sep 93 09:38:07 BST
From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Non-lethal weapons (was Re: Apologies)

Michael Morgan writes:
>We do really need a stunner of some kind. There are various chemical
>weapons around but these are unreliable. There is an article in a
>recent THE FUTURIST which relates to nonlethal weapons.
>
>We can make lethal weapons almost totally reliable. But effective,
>reliable non-lethal weapons may take decades to develop.

The government already has one: the police and penal system.  Ok, it does
kill some people, but that's going to be a problem with any supposedly
non-lethal weapon.  The line between immobilising and injuring or killing
is a fine one, and "decades" to develop a reliable, reliably non-lethal
weapon seems to me a great underestimate.  People can seriously injure
themselves just by fainting on a concrete floor.

What problem is a non-lethal weapon supposed to solve, anyway?  Who is this
"we" that needs a stunner?  Most defensive uses of handguns don't actually
result in deaths, or even injuries.

A non-lethal weapon would also make a dandy tool for kidnapping and
no-knock searches.  Avoids the PR problems of killing innocent people.

