Tim Starr writes:
>My statement was misleading: "Reality is what we perceive."  Perhaps I would
>have done better to formulate it as: "What we perceive is reality.  The
>things we perceive are real."

Presumably the "is" is to be taken in the "is a subset of" meaning, but in
that case, what is the argument about?  I expect we all agree that
perceptions and our interpretations of them exist, and that they form a
part but not the whole of reality, and that a perception one interprets as
the absence of a bus does not logically imply the absence of a bus,
notwithstanding that in practice it happens to be fairly reliable.

Or is the assertion that "The things we perceive are real" intended to
imply some further degree of reality to our perceptions, over and above the
obvious fact that they exist as perceptions?

